W("Reference to context: 'Richie was my whole life after his father died when Richie was in third grade.' (i) Who says this? (ii) What does it show about the mother-son relationship? (iii) How did she help him?", 3, "(i) Ebright's mother. (1) (ii) She devoted herself to him; they were very close. (1) (iii) She provided learning activities, equipment and trips, and brought home friends. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'I didn't get any real results. But I went ahead and showed that I had tried the experiment. This time I won.' (i) Which project is meant? (ii) Why did he win? (iii) What lesson did he learn?", 3, "(i) His eighth-grade project to find the cause of the viral disease that kills monarch caterpillars, by testing whether beetles carry it. (1) (ii) Because he did a real experiment, even though it gave no result. (1) (iii) That science is about method and honest trying, not just a neat display. (1)", "M"),
W("Reference to context: 'Richard wasn't interested in winning for winning's sake or winning to get a prize.' (i) Who said this? (ii) What did Ebright want? (iii) What ingredient of a scientist is described?", 3, "(i) Mr Weiherer. (1) (ii) To do the best job he could, to be best 'for the right reasons'. (1) (iii) The will to win for the right reasons. (1)", "M"),
W("Describe how Ebright's butterfly project grew from tagging to research on a hormone. Use four points.", 3, "He tagged monarchs for Dr Urquhart; he wrote to him for ideas after losing a fair; he studied the purpose of the gold spots on the monarch pupa and showed that they produced a hormone needed for development; later he identified its chemical structure at the Department of Agriculture laboratory. (3)", "M"),
W("Why did Ebright lose at the seventh-grade science fair, and how did he improve next time?", 3, "His entry was just a neat display of frog tissue slides. The winners had done real experiments. He then chose the subject he knew best, insects, wrote to Dr Urquhart for experiments, and began real experiments. (3)", "M"),
W("Explain the theory of mimicry that Ebright tested with a starling.", 3, "Viceroy butterflies look like monarchs; monarchs taste bad to birds while viceroys taste good, so the more they look like monarchs, the less likely a bird will eat them. Ebright found that the starling would not eat ordinary food but would eat all the monarchs it could get. (3)", "M"),
W("How did Ebright get the idea for his theory on cell life? Who helped him write it up?", 3, "In his junior year at Harvard he saw X-ray photos of the chemical structure of a hormone; with his insect hormone findings they seemed to answer how a cell can 'read' the blueprint of its DNA. He worked all night with room-mate James R. Wong, drawing pictures and building plastic models; they wrote the paper together. (3)", "M"),
W("Vocabulary in context: 'Eureka!' - what does it mean and why does the writer say Ebright didn't shout it?", 2, "'Eureka' is a cry of triumph at a discovery, originally attributed to Archimedes. Ebright did not shout it or 'I've got it'; he quietly believed the photos gave him the answer, showing his calm temperament. (2)", "M"),
W("Vocabulary in context: Match: (a) entomology (b) county (c) starling (d) canoeist with (1) region (2) a person who paddles a light boat (3) study of insects (4) a common bird which is a good mimic.", 2, "(a)-(3), (b)-(1), (c)-(4), (d)-(2). (all four = 2; two or three correct = 1)", "M"),
W("Vocabulary in context: 'Tagging butterflies is tedious.' What does 'tedious' mean? Give a similar example from school life.", 2, "'Tedious' means tiresome because it is long and dull. Copying a long list of words many times is tedious. (2)", "M"),
W("Grammar in context: Change to indirect speech: His mother said, 'I was his only companion until he started school.'", 2, "His mother said that she had been his only companion until he started school. (2)", "M"),
