 ("table", ["Point", "Detail"], [["Genre", "Biographical article / profile"], ["Subject", "Richard H. Ebright"], ["Setting", "Reading, Pennsylvania; high school science fairs; Harvard University"], ["Key book", "The Travels of Monarch X"], ["Theme", "Curiosity, effort, encouragement and the will to win for the right reasons"]]),
 ("h2", "2. Summary in order"),
 ("ol", [
  "At twenty-two Ebright and his college room-mate published a new theory on how cells work in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Science, the first time the journal had published the work of college students. It all began with butterflies.",
  "An only child, Ebright collected butterflies from kindergarten, along with rocks, fossils, coins and stars. His mother encouraged him, bought telescopes, microscopes and cameras, and gave him learning work in the evenings. His father had died when he was in the third grade.",
  "By second grade he had collected all twenty-five species of butterflies near Reading. Then his mother got him *The Travels of Monarch X*, which told how monarchs migrate to Central America and invited readers to help tag butterflies for Dr Frederick A. Urquhart of the University of Toronto.",
  "Ebright tagged butterflies and raised thousands of monarchs in his basement. He later lost interest because tagging was tedious: only two tagged butterflies were ever recaptured, within seventy-five miles.",
  "In seventh grade he lost at the county science fair with a display of frog tissue slides and learned that winners did real experiments. Dr Urquhart sent a stack of experiment ideas. In eighth grade he tested whether a beetle carried a viral disease of monarch caterpillars; he won though he got no real results. Next he showed that a starling would eat monarchs, supporting the idea that viceroys copy monarchs.",
  "In high school he studied the twelve gold spots on a monarch pupa and showed with a built device that they produced a hormone needed for full development. He won at the International Fair and worked in laboratories (Walter Reed Army Institute of Research and the U.S. Department of Agriculture). As a senior he grew cells from a monarch's wing and showed that they divided only when fed the hormone.",
  "At Harvard he identified the hormone's chemical structure, and in his junior year X-ray photos led him to his theory of how a cell reads the DNA blueprint. With room-mate James R. Wong he wrote the paper. He graduated second in a class of 1,510 and began work at Harvard Medical School. The theory might help prevent some cancers and other diseases.",
  "Ebright was also a champion debater, a canoeist and a photographer. His teacher Mr Weiherer said that he wanted to win 'for the right reasons'. The writer sums up: a first-rate mind, curiosity and the will to win for the right reasons make a scientist."]),
